'''Rayo Vallecano de Madrid, S.A.D.''' ({{IPA|es|ˈraʝo βaʎeˈkano ðe maˈðɾið}}),{{efn|In isolation, ''Vallecano'' is pronounced {{IPA|es|baʎeˈkano|}}.}} often abbreviated to '''Rayo''' (Spanish for "thunderbolt"), is a Spanish professional football club based in [[Madrid]], in the neighbourhood of [[Vallecas]]. The club competes in [[La Liga]], the top flight of [[Spanish football league system|Spanish football]].

Founded on 29 May 1924, the club is known for its sociocultural tradition, recognized for representing the [[barrio]]-local culture and its [[Working class|working-class]] status. Its home matches has been played at the 14,708-capacity [[Campo de Fútbol de Vallecas]] stadium since 1976.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.rayovallecano.es/club/estadio|title=Estadio de Vallecas {{!}} Rayo - Web Oficial|website=Estadio de Vallecas {{!}} Rayo - Web Oficial|language=es|access-date=2020-01-18|archive-date=2017-07-08|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170708210040/http://www.rayovallecano.es/club/estadio|url-status=live}}</ref>

During its history, Rayo has spent 19 seasons in the top-flight, and have played in one European competition, the [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] in the [[2000–01 UEFA Cup|2000–01 season]]. The club won the [[2017–18 Segunda División]]. By historical performance, Rayo is the third best club in Community of Madrid, after [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] and [[Atlético Madrid]].

==History==
===Establishment and early years===
Rayo Vallecano was founded on 29 May 1924 in the hometown of Prudencia Priego, wife of the club's first president Julián Huerta. Greatly inspired by [[Club Atlético River Plate|River Plate]] (a Football club from [[Argentina]]), in 1949, after an agreement with [[Atlético Madrid]], a red diagonal stripe was added to the team's kit, and the club reached [[Tercera División]] for the first time in its history.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rayovallecano.es/historia/|title=Historia resumida del Rayo|trans-title=Brief history of Rayo|publisher=Rayo Vallecano|language=es|access-date=4 April 2014|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140407061142/http://www.rayovallecano.es/historia/|archive-date=7 April 2014}}</ref>

===Yo-yo years===
One of the perennial yo-yo clubs of Spanish football, and always in the shadow of the two biggest clubs in the city ([[Real Madrid C.F.|Real Madrid]] and [[Atlético Madrid]]), Rayo Vallecano spent many years during the 1980s and 1990s moving back and forth between [[La Liga]] and [[Segunda División]]. The 1983–84 season was the worst during the 1980s. The club finished in the last position in Segunda División and was relegated to Segunda División B.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bdfutbol.com/en/t/t1983-842a.html|title=Classification 2nd Division 1983-84|website=www.bdfutbol.com|access-date=2019-11-05|archive-date=2019-04-25|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190425053214/https://www.bdfutbol.com/en/t/t1983-842a.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Due to a tragedy Rayo Vallecano turned out to be [[Laurie Cunningham]]'s last club; he was killed in a car crash just outside Madrid in 1989, after a sole season. He had recently won an [[F.A. Cup]] winners medal with [[Wimbledon F.C.]] in England the previous year and had also represented neighbours Real Madrid for four years.

They appeared to have consolidated their top flight status after [[1998–99 Segunda División|gaining promotion in 1999]], and the team's most successful season came in [[2000–01 La Liga|2000–01]] when they [[2000–01 UEFA Cup|reached the quarter-finals]] of the [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]], going out only to eventual runners-up [[Deportivo Alavés|Alavés]];<ref>{{cite web|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/uefa_cup/1220513.stm|title=Alaves through as Rayo fall|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|date=15 March 2001|access-date=4 April 2014|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140407101012/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/uefa_cup/1220513.stm|archive-date=7 April 2014}}</ref> Rayo finished ninth in [[1999–2000 La Liga|the previous campaign]], but entered the competition via the [[UEFA Fair Play ranking|fair play draw]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://deportes.terra.es/valladolid/el-fair-play-una-puerta-abierta-para-jugar-en-europa,df54797edf78d310VgnVCM3000009acceb0aRCRD.html|title=El 'Fair Play', ¿una puerta abierta para jugar en Europa?|trans-title='Fair Play', open door to play in Europe?|publisher=Terra|language=es|date=20 March 2013|access-date=4 April 2014|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140407101328/http://deportes.terra.es/valladolid/el-fair-play-una-puerta-abierta-para-jugar-en-europa,df54797edf78d310VgnVCM3000009acceb0aRCRD.html|archive-date=7 April 2014}}</ref>

===2003–11: Segunda División and below===

[[File:Deportivorayo.JPG|thumb|320px|[[Deportivo de La Coruña]] vs. Rayo Vallecano]]

However, the club shortly thereafter fell on hard times, enduring successive relegations in [[2002–03 La Liga|2003]] and [[2003–04 Segunda División|2004]]. For [[2005–06 Segunda División B|2005–06]] manager [[Míchel (footballer, born 1963)|Míchel]], a Real Madrid legend in the 1980s and '90s, was hired.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.abc.es/hemeroteca/historico-23-06-2005/abc/Ultima/michel-nuevo-entrenador-del-rayo_203335437757.html|title=Michel, nuevo entrenador del Rayo|trans-title=Michel, new Rayo manager|publisher=[[ABC (newspaper)|ABC]]|language=es|date=23 June 2005|access-date=4 April 2014|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140409135213/http://www.abc.es/hemeroteca/historico-23-06-2005/abc/Ultima/michel-nuevo-entrenador-del-rayo_203335437757.html|archive-date=9 April 2014}}</ref>

Rayo finished the [[2006–07 Segunda División B|2006–07 season]] in second place in [[Segunda División B]], winning the [[2007 Segunda División B play-offs|promotion play-off]] semifinal but losing in the final to [[SD Eibar|Eibar]] (1–2 aggregate).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://futbol.as.com/futbol/2007/06/24/mas_futbol/1182636012_850215.html|title=El Eibar regresa a Segunda tras remontar ante el Rayo Vallecano|trans-title=Eibar returns to ''Segunda'' after coming back from behind against Rayo Vallecano|publisher=[[Diario AS]]|language=es|date=24 June 2007|access-date=4 April 2014|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140407093633/http://futbol.as.com/futbol/2007/06/24/mas_futbol/1182636012_850215.html|archive-date=7 April 2014}}</ref> [[2007–08 Segunda División B|The following campaign]], the team returned to division two after a four-year absence after a victorious run in [[2008 Segunda División B play-offs|the playoffs]], disposing of [[Benidorm CF|Benidorm]] in the semi-final and [[Zamora CF|Zamora]] in the last game 2–1 on aggregate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://archivo.marca.com/edicion/marca/futbol/2a_division/rayo_vallecano/es/desarrollo/1135303.html|title=El Rayo vuelve a la División de Plata del fútbol español|trans-title=Rayo return to silver category of Spanish football|publisher=[[Marca (newspaper)|Marca]]|language=es|date=15 June 2008|access-date=4 April 2014|url-status=live|archive-url=http://archive.wikiwix.com/cache/20140514094300/http://archivo.marca.com/edicion/marca/futbol/2a_division/rayo_vallecano/es/desarrollo/1135303.html|archive-date=14 May 2014}}</ref>

In its first seasons back in the second tier of Spanish football, Rayo finished comfortably, often either in or just outside the promotion places. In [[2010–11 Segunda División|2010–11]], the team ranked in second position and returned to the top flight after an eight-year absence, only trailing champions [[Real Betis]] in spite of very serious economic problems.<ref>[http://www.footballscouting.org/2011/03/dona-teresa-takes-off-mask.html Dona Teresa takes off mask] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110426202631/http://www.footballscouting.org/2011/03/dona-teresa-takes-off-mask.html |date=2011-04-26 }}; Football Scouting, 1 March 2011</ref><ref>[http://in.reuters.com/article/idINIndia-55977820110330 Unpaid Rayo have sights set on La Liga payday] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121004193132/http://in.reuters.com/article/2011/03/30/idINIndia-55977820110330 |date=2012-10-04 }}; [[Reuters]], 30 March 2011</ref><ref>[http://www.elcorreogallego.es/deportes/futbol/ecg/jugadores-rayo-vallecano-seguiran-cobrar/idEdicion-2011-02-26/idNoticia-643771/ Los jugadores del Rayo Vallecano seguirán sin cobrar (Rayo Vallecano players will still not be paid)] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110927155248/http://www.elcorreogallego.es/deportes/futbol/ecg/jugadores-rayo-vallecano-seguiran-cobrar/idEdicion-2011-02-26/idNoticia-643771/ |date=2011-09-27 }}; [[El Correo Gallego]], 26 February 2011 {{in lang|es}}</ref>

===2011–: La Liga and Segunda División yo-yo===
[[File:Diego Costa 2012 Rayo Vallecano.jpg|thumb|299x299px|[[Diego Costa]] with Rayo Vallecano in 2012]]
[[File:Rayo Vallecano de Madrid league performance 1929-2023.svg|thumb|Chart of Rayo Vallecano league performance 1929–2023]]
In March 2014, [[Huawei]] agreed to sponsor Rayo Vallecano for two league matches against Real Madrid and [[Athletic Bilbao]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://gsminsider.com/2014/03/huawei-rayo-vallecano-shirt-sponsor-real-madrid-ath-bilbao/|title=Huawei sponsors Rayo Vallecano for two matches, against Real Madrid and Bilbao|publisher=GSM Insider|date=30 March 2014|access-date=30 March 2014|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140330064350/http://gsminsider.com/2014/03/huawei-rayo-vallecano-shirt-sponsor-real-madrid-ath-bilbao/|archive-date=30 March 2014}}</ref>

In August 2015, Rayo Vallecano purchased the majority of [[Oklahoma City FC (NASL)|Oklahoma City FC]], a [[North American Soccer League (2011–2017)|NASL]] expansion franchise which had yet to officially play a game renaming the club to [[Rayo OKC]], despite the stadium increasingly needing work. It was the first ever entry of a Spanish club into the American sports market and mirrored a 2013 sponsorship agreement with [[Qbao]] in terms of expanding the club's profile overseas.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://thesportsdaily.com/2015/08/22/rayo-vallecano-set-to-buy-oklahoma-city-fc/|title=Rayo Vallecano set to buy Oklahoma City FC|date=22 August 2015 |access-date=2018-06-02|archive-date=2021-03-05|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210305231501/https://thesportsdaily.com/2015/08/22/rayo-vallecano-set-to-buy-oklahoma-city-fc/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://futbol.as.com/futbol/2015/08/19/primera/1439982748_769849.html|title=El Rayo compra la mayoría de acciones del Oklahoma City|language=es|trans-title=Rayo purchases majority of Oklahoma City shares|publisher=[[Diario AS|AS]]|date=19 August 2015|access-date=21 August 2015|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150820122343/http://futbol.as.com/futbol/2015/08/19/primera/1439982748_769849.html|archive-date=20 August 2015}}</ref> Rayo OKC folded after a year due to Rayo Vallecano's relegation from La Liga and a dispute between the co-owners led to less finance for the U.S. side.

In May 2016, Rayo Vallecano were relegated to the Segunda División, finishing 18th in the [[2015–16 La Liga]] season. This ended their five-year streak in La Liga, their longest ever stay in the top-flight.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.skysports.com/football/news/11827/10283933/la-liga-getafe-and-rayo-vallecano-relegated-sporting-gijon-stay-up|title=La Liga: Getafe and Rayo Vallecano relegated, Sporting Gijon stay up|access-date=2018-06-02|archive-date=2018-06-16|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180616053425/http://www.skysports.com/football/news/11827/10283933/la-liga-getafe-and-rayo-vallecano-relegated-sporting-gijon-stay-up|url-status=live}}</ref> Their first season back in the second division was a poor one, with both problems on the field and off, and they finished in 12th position. Rayo went through three managers in the [[2016–17 Segunda División]] season before finally settling on club legend [[Míchel (footballer, born 1975)|Míchel]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.laliga.es/en/news/michel-has-been-appointed-the-new-coach-of-rayo-vallecano|title=Míchel has been appointed the new coach of Rayo Vallecano|access-date=2018-06-02|archive-date=2017-09-21|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170921192801/http://www.laliga.es/en/news/michel-has-been-appointed-the-new-coach-of-rayo-vallecano|url-status=live}}</ref> He revived the club from the relegation places to 12th, almost making the playoffs.

At the start of the [[2017–18 Segunda División]] season, the club appointed their recently retired goalkeeper [[David Cobeño]] as the sporting director of the club.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.elcomercio.es/deportes/futbol/201706/22/david-cobeno-nuevo-director-20170622003249-v.html|title=David Cobeño, new sports director|access-date=2018-06-02|archive-date=2018-07-26|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180726170059/https://www.elcomercio.es/deportes/futbol/201706/22/david-cobeno-nuevo-director-20170622003249-v.html|url-status=live}}</ref> They secured their promotion with a 1–0 over [[CD Lugo]] with one game remaining.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fourfourtwo.com/news/rayo-vallecano-win-promotion-laliga|title=Rayo Vallecano win promotion to La Liga|date=27 May 2018 |access-date=2018-06-02|archive-date=2018-07-26|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180726134930/https://www.fourfourtwo.com/news/rayo-vallecano-win-promotion-laliga|url-status=live}}</ref> That season the club won Segunda División with 76 points in 42 games.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.resultados-futbol.com/segunda2018/grupo1/jornada42|title=Jornada 42 de Segunda División, Temporada 2017/2018 - liga smartbank, segunda division, campeonato nacional de liga de segunda división, segunda division española, laliga 2 española|website=www.resultados-futbol.com|access-date=2019-12-26|archive-date=2019-12-26|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191226005638/http://www.resultados-futbol.com/segunda2018/grupo1/jornada42|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Real Valladolid - Rayo Vallecano 2019-01-05 20.jpg|thumb|Rayo players during an away La Liga fixture versus [[Real Valladolid]] in January 2019]]
On 20 March 2019, the club appointed [[Paco Jémez]] as head coach,<ref>{{Cite web|title=Paco Jémez nuevo entrenador del Rayo Vallecano|url=http://www.rayovallecano.es/noticia/paco-jemez-nuevo-entrenador-del-rayo-vallecano|access-date=2021-10-28|website=Rayo Vallecano|language=es|archive-date=2020-09-28|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200928180504/http://www.rayovallecano.es/noticia/paco-jemez-nuevo-entrenador-del-rayo-vallecano|url-status=live}}</ref>  and on 4 May, Rayo was relegated back to the Segunda División after losing 4–1 to [[Levante UD]], eventually finishing last<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.football-espana.net/78089/rayo-vallecano-relegated-segunda-division/|title=Rayo Vallecano relegated to the Segunda Division|website=Football Espana|access-date=2019-05-07|archive-date=2019-05-07|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190507143821/https://www.football-espana.net/78089/rayo-vallecano-relegated-segunda-division|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.resultados-futbol.com/primera2019/grupo1|title=Primera División, Temporada 2018/2019|website=www.resultados-futbol.com|access-date=2019-12-26|archive-date=2019-11-01|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191101020012/http://www.resultados-futbol.com/primera2019/grupo1|url-status=live}}</ref>

In August 2020, the club appointed [[Andoni Iraola]] as head coach.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Andoni Iraola nuevo entrenador del Rayo Vallecano|url=http://www.rayovallecano.es/noticia/andoni-iraola-nuevo-entrenador-del-rayo-vallecano|access-date=2021-10-27|website=Rayo Vallecano {{!}} Rayo|language=es|archive-date=2021-10-27|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211027145249/http://www.rayovallecano.es/noticia/andoni-iraola-nuevo-entrenador-del-rayo-vallecano|url-status=live}}</ref> They finished sixth and won promotion in the playoffs against [[Girona FC]]; despite losing the first leg at home 1–2, the team came back to win the second leg 2–0 away to claim a place in La Liga for [[2021–22 La Liga|2021–22]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Brennan|first=Feargal|date=2021-06-20|title=WATCH: 10-man Rayo Vallecano secure La Liga promotion with battling Girona win|url=https://www.football-espana.net/2021/06/20/watch-10-man-rayo-vallecano-secure-la-liga-promotion-with-battling-girona-win|access-date=2021-10-27|website=Football Espana|archive-date=2021-10-27|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211027145251/https://www.football-espana.net/2021/06/20/watch-10-man-rayo-vallecano-secure-la-liga-promotion-with-battling-girona-win|url-status=live}}</ref> In February 2022, Iraola's side defeated [[RCD Mallorca]] to make the semi-finals of the Copa del Rey; it was the second time in club history and first since 1982.<ref>{{cite news |title=El Rayo de Iraola hace historia en Copa Del Rey al volver 40 años después a semis |trans-title=Iraola's Rayo make history in the Copa del Rey by returning to the semis after 40 years |url=https://www.marca.com/radio/2022/02/02/61faf25a22601dcf3a8b45e6.html |access-date=2 February 2022 |work=Marca |date=2 February 2022 |language=Spanish |archive-date=2 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220202211829/https://www.marca.com/radio/2022/02/02/61faf25a22601dcf3a8b45e6.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The club finished 12th in La Liga. This was a big achievement as they were by far the leagues smallest team, and most had predicted that they would be relegated.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Lowe |first=Sid |date=2021-09-20 |title=The Tiger who came for free: Falcao is back and scoring in La Liga |language=en-GB |work=The Guardian |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2021/sep/20/the-tiger-who-came-for-free-radamel-falcao-is-back-and-scoring-in-la-liga-rayo-vallecano |access-date=2023-02-11 |issn=0261-3077 |archive-date=2023-02-11 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230211001223/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2021/sep/20/the-tiger-who-came-for-free-radamel-falcao-is-back-and-scoring-in-la-liga-rayo-vallecano |url-status=live }}</ref>

